WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.600
CSF 2.0 is most useful when it improves decisions.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.620
It loses value when it becomes checkbox theater:

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.960
a paperwork exercise where teams mark outcomes as complete without understanding scope,

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.820
evidence, ownership, or risk.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.240
Checkbox behavior can create a false sense of maturity.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.740
It may satisfy a meeting agenda, but it does not necessarily improve identity management,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.600
logging, incident readiness, supplier oversight, or recovery capability.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.220
Common mistakes include copying another organization's profile without tailoring it,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.800
assigning no owners, ignoring business context, collecting evidence that nobody reviews,

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.540
or confusing framework

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.000
alignment with real control operation.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:49.100
Another mistake is treating CSF 2.0 as a certification or guarantee.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:50.680
It is neither.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:55.620
It supports risk management, but the organization still has to design controls,

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.000
operate them, validate them, and make transparent risk decisions.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.540
Practical application starts with scope.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.320
What environment, product, business unit, supplier relationship,

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.460
or critical service is being assessed?

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.480
Next, define stakeholders and owners.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.140
Connect CSF outcomes to real risks and business objectives.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.160
Decide which outcomes belong in the Target Profile.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.820
Compare the Current Profile to the target, prioritize gaps,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.600
link work to controls or procedures, and document the improvement plan.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.420
Evidence should be useful.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.800
It should help an owner and decision maker understand whether an outcome is operating,

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.580
partially operating, or not yet achieved.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.200
Exceptions should be documented honestly with risk decisions and review dates.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:46.780
Profiles should be updated as systems, suppliers, threats, and priorities change.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.840
The framework should make risk communication cleaner and improvement more deliberate,

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.820
not simply create documents.
